VIF1: a fast path for plain UNPACKs into VU1 data memory. An UNPACK without a write mask, without row addition (MODE 0, or 3, which the general loop also treats as none), with CL >= WL and 8, 16 or 32 bits per component (not V4-5) is written by a loop made for its format: the same vectors to the same addresses in the same order as the general loop (S broadcasts, V2 writes x y x y, V3 keeps w, destinations wrap at 1024 quadwords), with no per-component format decisions. PS2X_VIF1_UNPACK_VERIFY=1 runs the general loop as well on the same memory and compares what both wrote. Checked in Metropolismania: 3.38 million plain UNPACKs in a 140 s city run with the check on, no difference; all 4 MB of VRAM byte-identical at the eleven views of a scripted city camera run with frame skip off; PS2Recomp's tests give the same results. processVIF1Data goes from 10.1% of the game thread to 2.2% (plus 1.3% in the new loop).

